  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      This is a government biometric programme, so what follows is deliberately limited to
      engineering technique. No system internals, schemas, vendors or operational details appear
      here — nothing beyond what is already on my public CV.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The situation</h2>

  <p>
    A national identity platform (ABIS) ingests biometric enrolments continuously. When I took over
    technical lead, the pipeline was sustaining roughly <strong>4,000 to 6,000 records an hour</strong>.
    That number was not catastrophic on its own — the problem was that intake was growing faster than
    processing, so the backlog compounded daily. A system that falls behind a little every day is on
    a schedule to fail.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two constraints shaped everything. First, this is identity data for a national population, so the
    security baseline was not negotiable to buy speed. Second, the platform runs across sixteen
    Kubernetes nodes spanning Production and Disaster Recovery, which meant any change had to behave
    identically in both.
  </p>

  <h2>What the platform actually is</h2>

  <p>
    It is worth being precise about the shape, because it changes what "optimisation" means. This is
    not one application with a slow function in it. It is <strong>nine independently deployed
    services and clients</strong> on .NET 8 — a core web API, an administration API, a user and
    access service, a biometric extraction service, an EFT import service with its own Angular
    client, a migration API, a WPF capture client for enrolment stations, and a load-testing harness
    built specifically to reproduce production pressure.
  </p>

  <p>
    PostgreSQL is authoritative behind EF Core and Npgsql, with S3-compatible object storage for
    biometric artefacts. Every endpoint is versioned, because the WPF clients are installed on
    machines in the field and cannot be redeployed in step with the server.
  </p>

  <p>
    A record therefore crosses process boundaries on its way through enrolment. That is precisely why
    measuring first mattered: in a distributed system the slow part is rarely where the person
    debugging it assumes.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring before changing anything</h2>

  <p>
    The tempting move was to start optimising the code that <em>looked</em> slow. I did not. We put
    OpenTelemetry tracing in first, exported over OTLP, with Grafana dashboards on the enrolment path.
    Until you can see where a record actually spends its time, optimisation is guesswork with a
    deployment risk attached.
  </p>

  <p>
    What the traces showed was that the bottleneck was not compute. It was the volume of small
    round-trips per record and the growing cost of writes against tables that had become very large.
  </p>

  <h2>What actually moved the number</h2>

  <h3>Request batching</h3>
  <p>
    The pipeline was processing records one at a time, paying fixed per-request overhead thousands of
    times an hour. Batching related work collapsed that overhead dramatically. This was the single
    largest contributor, and it cost nothing in correctness because the operations were already
    independent.
  </p>

  <h3>Query and index optimisation</h3>
  <p>
    Several hot queries were doing more work than they needed — scanning where they could seek,
    and returning columns nobody read. Reworking those queries and the indexes behind them removed
    a large, steady tax that applied to every single record.
  </p>

  <h3>Table partitioning</h3>
  <p>
    The deeper problem was that write cost was climbing as tables grew — the system was getting
    slower precisely because it was succeeding. Partitioning the large tables meant writes landed on
    smaller, hotter partitions, so throughput stopped degrading as the dataset expanded. This is the
    change that made the gain durable rather than temporary.
  </p>

  <div class="result">
    <div class="figure">23,000 records / hour</div>
    <p>
      Up from 4,000–6,000 — roughly a fourfold increase, verified in Grafana against production
      traffic rather than a synthetic benchmark.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Not trading correctness for speed</h2>

  <p>
    A faster pipeline that loses or double-processes records is worse than a slow one. Alongside the
    throughput work, the ingestion path was built to be resilient by design:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>RabbitMQ producer/consumer</strong> to decouple intake from processing, so a slow
      downstream stage applies back-pressure instead of dropping work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Transactional outbox dispatch</strong>, so a record is never committed locally without
      its corresponding message eventually being published — no lost events on a crash between the
      two.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lease-based job recovery</strong>, so work claimed by a node that dies is picked up by
      another rather than stranded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Polly retry and circuit-breaker policies</strong> on external calls, so a failing
      dependency degrades the system instead of cascading through it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The security baseline stayed put</h2>

  <p>
    Performance work is where security quietly erodes — a cache here, a skipped check there. I set the
    baseline explicitly so it could not drift, and it is layered rather than single-factor:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>OpenIddict</strong> issuing and validating tokens, with <strong>Keycloak</strong>
      providing SSO and MFA for operators.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mutual-TLS certificate authentication</strong> between services, so a service cannot be
      impersonated from inside the cluster.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>HashiCorp Vault PKI with AppRole</strong> managing those certificates and the secrets
      behind them, rather than configuration files holding long-lived credentials.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>AES-256-GCM/JWE envelope encryption</strong> for data at rest.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>None of it was relaxed to reach the throughput target.</p>

  <h2>Making it hold</h2>

  <p>
    Throughput gains decay if the next change can silently undo them. We ended with
    <strong>44 xUnit suites</strong> covering end-to-end flows, idempotency, and architecture rules —
    the last of those failing the build when code crosses a layer boundary it should not — plus
    FluentValidation on the request surface and health checks on the database and every downstream
    dependency. Combined with the OpenTelemetry tracing already in place, a regression shows up as a
    failing test or a visible dashboard change rather than a support ticket three weeks later.
  </p>

  <p>
    GitLab CI/CD runs separate pipelines for Test, Production and Disaster Recovery. DR being a real
    pipeline rather than a documented procedure is the part that matters: a recovery environment
    nobody deploys to is a recovery environment nobody can trust.
  </p>

  <h2>What I would tell another engineer</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Instrument first.</strong> The bottleneck was not where the team assumed it was, and we
      would have spent weeks optimising the wrong layer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fixed overhead beats clever algorithms.</strong> Batching away per-request cost
      outperformed anything subtle we could have done inside the processing itself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask whether the fix survives growth.</strong> Query tuning gave an immediate win;
      partitioning is what stopped the win from eroding as the data grew.
    </li>
  </ul>
